            <ArticleTitle>Complementary and alternative treatments for primary dysmenorrhea in adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-12</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/01.NPR.0000454984.19413.28</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Primary dysmenorrhea is the most common gynecologic complaint among adolescents. Conventional treatments include nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and hormonal contraceptives, but complementary and alternative medicine is a growing area of interest. As patients seek such treatments, pediatric nurse practitioners should be aware of these options to offer the best advice to patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
